gvproxy forwards every guest connection to wherever the guest points it,
including the host: its loopback, once the NAT mapping is gone, still
answers on every interface address, so a guest reaches whatever the host
binds on 0.0.0.0 or on its LAN address. The forwards of `stack.forwards`
bind whatever address the config names, so a typo publishes the guest to
the LAN.

This adds two switches to the stack configuration and the command line.
`denyHostAccess` / `-deny-host-access` refuses TCP and UDP from the guest
to loopback, to the unspecified address, and to every address of a host
interface; the interface list is re-read every ten seconds, and while it
cannot be read every destination is refused. `loopbackForwardsOnly` /
`-loopback-forwards-only` rejects a TCP or UDP forward that binds a
non-loopback address. Applies on top of gvproxy-guest-isolation.patch.
packages/qemu-vm sets both. Drop this patch when upstream ships the
switches.

